# Bank 7 (runs at 0x8000). Directives: L label, C line comment, B block
# header, D data range, F row width.
#
# THIS IS THE SOUND PLAYER. The interrupt (0x4028 in bank 0) puts this bank in
# 0x8000 and bank 8 in 0xA000, calls 0x8063, and restores the previous split
# by reading it from 0xF0F1..0xF0F3. Everything that sounds comes from here,
# and all of it is written to the PSG through the BIOS: WRTPSG (0x0093) and
# RDPSG (0x0096).
#
# Only 794 bytes of the bank are reachable code; the rest is data, indexed by
# the table of 80 pointers at 0x8328.
B 0x8000 A CHANNEL IS A CARD OF 0x11 BYTES AND A STREAM OF COMMANDS
B 0x8000 The player keeps three identical cards, at 0xE010, 0xE021 and 0xE032,
B 0x8000 of 0x11 bytes each: byte 0 is the countdown of the note that is
B 0x8000 sounding, 1 its length, 2 the mode, 3 and 4 where its command stream
B 0x8000 has got to, 5 the switches (tone, noise and envelope), 6 the octave
B 0x8000 shift, 7 to 9 the volume and its decay, 0x0B the time unit, 0x0C the
B 0x8000 sharp, 0x0D and 0x0E the envelope times, and 0x0F and 0x10 the last
B 0x8000 period written. Everything that sounds comes from reading those
B 0x8000 streams byte by byte.
L 0x8000 repeat_stream   Command 0xFE: the word after it says where to go back to, and byte 10 counts the laps done
C 0x8000 Byte 10: the laps done so far
C 0x8005 Against the ones the command asks for
C 0x800C One more lap
C 0x8010 And the stream goes back to wherever the word says
L 0x801B laps_done   The counter to zero and the stream carries on after the command
C 0x801E The laps, to zero
C 0x8024 Byte 0 goes up by one: the current note ends right now
L 0x802A set_mix   Builds PSG register 7: for each channel, whether tone sounds, whether noise sounds, or neither
C 0x802A 0xE043: the current mix
C 0x802E The two low bits of byte 5: tone and noise
C 0x8034 The channel number
C 0x803B Bit 1: this channel's noise
C 0x8046 The noise bits are three bits higher up
C 0x8049 And bit 0: its tone
C 0x8053 The new mix, noted down
C 0x8057 PSG register 7
L 0x805C clear_that_bit   The bit set to zero: that channel sounds
L 0x8060 set_that_bit   The bit set to one: that channel is silent
L 0x8063 play   What the interrupt calls every frame (p00:4035), with banks 7 and 8 in place.
C 0x8063 0xE047: while the noise effect is running, it is in charge
C 0x806A 0xE04C to one: when it ends the registers must be restored
C 0x806F Mix 0xB8: the three tones silenced and noise on C
C 0x8073 WRTPSG with A=7: the mixer register, the one that decides which channels sound and which put out noise.
C 0x8076 0xE048: the first frame of the effect
C 0x807D The four bytes of the count: 0, 7, 5 and 0x10
C 0x8088 The three channels silenced...
C 0x808B ...and period 0xD6 in A and B
L 0x808D set_tone_a_and_b   Writes PSG registers 0, 2, 1 and 3: the periods of channels A and B.
C 0x808E Register 0: the low byte of A's period
C 0x8092 And 2: B's
C 0x8097 Registers 1 and 3, the high bytes, to zero
L 0x80A3 run_noise_effect   Counts down the effect's two counters: the inner one keeps taking volume away and the outer one changes the noise register
C 0x80A3 0xE04A: only with the effect on
C 0x80A9 The inner counter
C 0x80AD One frame in two
C 0x80B0 0xE04B: one volume step less
L 0x80BB next_noise_step   Once the outer counter runs out, the effect moves on to the next register value from 0x80D9; when they run out, the channels go silent
C 0x80BC The outer counter
C 0x80BD And when it runs out, the effect is over
C 0x80C2 Six frames until the next step
C 0x80C8 The table at 0x80D9: the period for each step
C 0x80D0 And the volume goes back to 0x0F
D 0x80D9 0x80DD noise_registers  Four bytes that 0x80C8 indexes with `ld hl,0x80D9 / add hl,de / ld e,(hl)`: the value handed to the PSG at each step of the effect.
F 0x80D9 4
L 0x80DD silence_channels   Sets E to zero and falls into 0x80E4, that is, sets the volume of the three channels to zero.
C 0x80DD Volume zero
C 0x80DF Register 10: C's volume
L 0x80E4 set_volume   Writes E to PSG registers 9 and 8: the volume of channels B and A.
C 0x80E4 Register 9: B's
C 0x80E9 And 8: A's
L 0x80ED restore_registers   When the noise effect ends, the four periods and the three volumes go back to what the channels had
C 0x80ED 0xE04C: only if the effect had overwritten the registers
C 0x80F4 And then there is nothing left to restore
C 0x80F8 The periods saved in the cards
C 0x8113 Channel A's volume...
C 0x811C ...B's...
C 0x8125 ...and C's
C 0x812E And whatever mix there was
L 0x8134 run_three_channels   The three 0x11-byte cards at 0xE010, one after another
C 0x8134 Channel A: register 1
C 0x8136 0xE010: the first card
C 0x813A 0xE044: the mute countdown
C 0x8145 0x60 frames per step
C 0x8149 Six steps and it goes completely silent
C 0x8151 The channel that carries 0xA9 has a card of its own
C 0x8167 0x11 bytes: the next card
C 0x816B Byte 2 at zero: this channel does not sound
C 0x8179 Two PSG registers per channel
L 0x8181 run_channel   A channel's interpreter: fetches the next command from the stream it points to (IX+3, IX+4). 0xFE and the codes above it are control commands; those below, note and length.
C 0x8181 Byte 2: the mode
C 0x8184 With bit 7 set, the channel takes another path
C 0x8189 Byte 0: the frames the note has left
C 0x818D Bytes 3 and 4: where the stream has got to
C 0x8193 The channel's command byte.
C 0x8194 0xFE is the command that jumps to the start of the bank.
C 0x8199 And above 0xFE, the channel goes silent
C 0x819F Bit 7 of the mode
C 0x81A5 Commands 0x2x are the switches
C 0x81AC Byte 5: tone, noise and envelope
C 0x81B1 And byte 1: the length
C 0x81B8 A bare 0x20 has nothing after it
C 0x81C1 Bit 3 of byte 5: it uses the envelope
C 0x81CB PSG registers 12 and 11: the envelope period.
C 0x81CF And 11, the low byte
C 0x81D7 Command 0x1x: the noise period
C 0x81DF Its low nibble, times two
C 0x81E5 Register 6: the noise period.
C 0x81E9 The high nibble: the note
C 0x81ED And the low one, with the byte after it: the period
L 0x81F1 set_note   Writes the period to the channel's two registers and starts the note's countdown
C 0x81F9 The high nibble, moved down: the volume
C 0x8201 (IX+1) is the length, and it is reloaded into (IX+0), which is the countdown.
L 0x8206 silence_this_channel   Byte 2 to zero, with no drawing and no switches
C 0x8206 Byte 2 to zero: the channel does not sound
L 0x8213 lower_one_more_step   One extra step in the decay
L 0x8216 lower_volume   Byte 8 goes down one at a time to zero
C 0x8216 Byte 8: the current volume
C 0x821A Not below zero
L 0x821F write_volume_and_mix   The mix, and the channel's volume (or the envelope, if it uses one)
C 0x821F The mix, in case it has changed
C 0x8222 This channel's volume register: 8, 9 or 10
C 0x8227 Bit 3 of byte 5: it uses the envelope
C 0x822E Register 13: the envelope shape
C 0x8233 And the volume with bit 4: the envelope is in charge
L 0x823B run_melody_channel   With bit 7 of the mode set, the note also fades out on its own: byte 9 says when it starts to decay
C 0x823B The frames the note has left
C 0x823E Run out: on to the next command
C 0x8241 Byte 9: when it starts to decay
C 0x824D Byte 14: the floor of the decay
L 0x8255 read_melody_command   Commands 0xDx, 0xFx and 0xEx set time, volume, envelope and octave; the last nibble is the note
C 0x8255 Command 0xDx: the time unit
C 0x825F Byte 11: how long one beat lasts
C 0x8264 Command 0xFx: the volume
C 0x826C Byte 7: the starting volume
C 0x8271 And the byte after it brings the two times
C 0x8277 The high nibble, to byte 13...
C 0x827B ...and the low one, to 14
C 0x8282 Command 0xEx: sharp or octave
C 0x8288 Bit 3: sharp
C 0x828C Byte 12: one semitone up
C 0x8292 And if not, byte 6: the octave shift
C 0x8297 The low nibble: how many beats it lasts
C 0x829F The time unit, added that many times
C 0x82A4 Byte 1: how long the note lasts
C 0x82AB The high nibble: the note
C 0x82B2 Note 0x0C is the rest
C 0x82B9 0xE044: while muting, the volume drops at once
C 0x82C9 Byte 8: the current volume
C 0x82D0 Byte 0: the note's countdown
C 0x82D3 And byte 9: when it starts to decay
C 0x82DB The table at 0x831E: the period of the twelve notes
C 0x82E6 Byte 6: for each octave, the period times two
C 0x82F0 Byte 12: the sharp raises the period by one
C 0x82F7 The channel's high register...
C 0x8302 ...and the low one, both saved in the card
C 0x8308 Bit 7 of the mode: in melody mode, it carries on along another path
C 0x830F Byte 5 to two: tone only
L 0x8316 save_pointer   Leaves in (IX+3, IX+4) where the channel's stream has got to.
D 0x831E 0x8328 descending_table  Ten bytes going downhill (0x6B, 0x65, 0x5F, 0x5A, 0x55, 0x50, 0x4C, 0x47, 0x43, 0x40), read by 0x82DB.
F 0x831E 10
D 0x8328 0x83C8 sound_table  Eighty words: the address of each sound. Read by 0x4A49 in bank 0 with `ld de,0x8328` and `call 0x4062` (DE += A), with A = sound number times two. Entry 0 (0x393C) is not a cartridge address: sound 0 does not exist. The last three point to 0xA3DE, the first filler byte of bank 8, that is, to an empty sound.
F 0x8328 w2
D 0x83C8 0xA000 sound_data  The melodies and the effects, one after another, in the order in which the table above names them: 0x83C8, 0x83F3, 0x8400, ... 0x9FBF. They continue in bank 8, which is mapped at the same time.
F 0x83C8 16
